description:
A random oracle supplies hardness without algebraic structure. Bennett and Gill (1981) showed \(\mathsf P^{\mathcal O}
e\mathsf{NP}^{\mathcal O}\) for random \(\mathcal O\), and \(\mathsf P^{\mathcal O}
e\mathsf{NP}^{\mathcal O}\cap\mathsf{coNP}^{\mathcal O}\) for a random permutation, which is structured because every output has one preimage. The random-function case stayed open until GIKKLS (STOC 2023) answered it classically under \(\mathsf{UP}
ot\subseteq\mathsf{RP}\). The quantum case matters more: the post-quantum candidates (LWE, SIS, codes, isogenies) give hard search or promise problems, not total languages with certificates on both sides.
